==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|reckin||dialectal}}
* {{alter|en|recken||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|rekenen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|recenian|t=to pay; arrange, dispose, reckon}} and {{m|ang|ġerecenian|t=to explain, recount, relate}}; both from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*rekanōną|t=to count, explain}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*rekanaz|t=swift, ready, prompt}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₃reǵ-|t=to make straight or right}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|rekkin||to ennumerate, mention, narrate, rehearse, count, calculate, compute}}, {{cog|stq|reekenje|t=to calculate, figure, reckon}}, {{cog|fy|rekkenje||to account, tally, calculate, figure}}, {{cog|nl|rekenen||to count, calculate, reckon}}, {{cog|nds-de|reken||to reckon}}, {{cog|de|rechnen||to count, reckon, calculate}}, {{cog|sv|räkna||to count, calculate, reckon}}, {{cog|is|reikna||to calculate}}, {{cog|la|rectus|t=straight, right}}. See also {{l|en|reck}}, {{l|en|reach}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹɛkən/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-reckon.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛkən}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To count; to enumerate; to number; also, to compute; to calculate.
#* {{RQ:King James Version|book=Leviticus|chapter=27|verse=18|passage=then the priest shall '''reckon''' unto him the money according to the years that remain}}
#*{{RQ:Addison Italy}}
#*: {I '''reckoned''' above two hundred and fifty on the outside of the church.
# To count as in a number, rank, or series; to estimate by rank or quality; to place by estimation; to account; to esteem; to repute.
#* {{RQ:King James Version|book=Isaiah|chapter=53|verse=12|passage=He was '''reckoned''' among the transgressors}}
#* '''1671''', {{w|John Milton}}, ''Samson Agonistes''
#*: For him I '''reckon''' not in high estate Whom long descent of birth, Or the sphere of fortune, raises
# To charge, attribute, or adjudge to one, as having a certain quality or value.
#* '''1611''', {{w|King James Version}}, Romans 4:9
#*: {{...}} faith was '''reckoned''' to Abraham for righteousness.
#* {{RQ:Hawthorne SL|10}}
#*: Without her eccentricities being '''reckoned''' to her for a crime.
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} To conclude, as by an enumeration and balancing of chances; hence, to [[think]]; to [[suppose]]; -- followed by an objective clause
#: ''I '''reckon''' he won't try that again.''
#* '''1611''', {{w|King James Version}}, Romans 8:18
#*: For I '''reckon''' that the sufferings of this present time are not worthy to be compared with the glory which shall be revealed in us.
#* '''1611''', {{w|King James Version}}, Romans 6:11
#*: Likewise '''reckon''' ye also yourselves to be dead indeed unto sin.
# To [[reckon with]] something or somebody or not, i.e to [[reckon without]] something or somebody: to [[take into account]], [[deal with]], [[consider]] or not, i.e. to [[misjudge]], [[ignore]], not take into account, not deal with, not consider or [[fail]] to consider; e.g. [[reckon without one's host]]
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make an enumeration or computation; to engage in numbering or computing.
# To come to an accounting; to [[draw up]] or [[settle]] [[account]]s; to examine and strike the balance of debt and credit; to adjust relations of desert or penalty.
#* {{rfdatek|en|Chaucer}}
#*: Parfay," sayst thou, sometime he '''reckon''' shall." 

